  <h2>Shutoffs and common failures</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Appliance</th><th>Shutoff</th><th>What usually fails</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Washing machine</td><td>Two valves on the wall behind it</td><td>Rubber fill hoses that burst; drain hose popping out of the standpipe; a clogged standpipe overflowing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dishwasher</td><td>Under the kitchen sink</td><td>Supply line connection, door gasket, drain hose, or a pump seal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refrigerator</td><td>Behind or below the fridge, or under the sink</td><td>Thin plastic ice maker line cracking or loosening; clogged defrost drain</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toilet</td><td>Oval handle on the wall behind it</td><td>Supply line, fill valve, cracked tank, wax ring at the base</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kitchen and bath sinks</td><td>Two valves under the sink</td><td>Braided supply lines, drain trap connections, garbage disposal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water softener or filter</td><td>Bypass valve on the unit</td><td>Fittings and the drain line</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Stopping an overflowing toilet</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Lift the tank lid</strong> and press the rubber flapper at the bottom of the tank closed, or lift the float so the fill valve stops.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the supply valve</strong> on the wall behind the toilet, turning it clockwise.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat the water by what's in it.</strong> Clean tank water is Category 1; an overflow with urine is Category 2; anything with feces, or sewage coming up from the drain, is Category 3 and calls for gloves, boots and disinfection.</li>
    <li><strong>Check below.</strong> On upper floors, look at the ceiling underneath for stains or dripping, and switch off any light fixture there.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Cleaning up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Unplug the appliance or switch off its breaker before touching it or the water around it.</li>
    <li>Mop and extract standing water, and pull the appliance out to dry behind and underneath it.</li>
    <li>Check under kitchen cabinets and behind toe kicks; water hides under cabinet bases and laminate flooring.</li>
    <li>Photograph the failed hose or line and keep it for your insurer.</li>
    <li>If water reached the ceiling below, wall cavities or a finished basement, get a professional moisture inspection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prevent the next one</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Upgrade hoses and lines.</strong> Braided stainless steel washer hoses and ice maker lines resist bursting. Even braided lines should be replaced periodically.</li>
    <li><strong>Leave room behind the washer</strong> so the hoses don't kink against the wall.</li>
    <li><strong>Add leak sensors</strong> on the floor by the washer, dishwasher, fridge, water heater and under sinks.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider an automatic shutoff valve</strong> that closes the main when a sensor detects water or flow runs too long.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn off the washer valves</strong> when you travel, or shut off the main for long trips.</li>
  </ul>
